==English==
{{wp}}

===Alternative forms===
* {{alt|en|per-centage||archaic}}

===Etymology===
From {{m|en|percent}}, from {{der|en|la|per centum||for every hundred}}, + {{af|en|-age|pos1=suffix indicating a rate}}.<ref>{{R:OED Online|pos=n|id=3947178937}}</ref>

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/pə(ɹ)ˈsɛntɪd͡ʒ/}}
** {{audio|en|En-us-percentage.ogg|a=US}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈpɚsɛnt(e)ɪd͡ʒ/|a=Philippines,nonstandard}}
* {{hyph|en|per|cent|age}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# The [[amount]], [[number]] or [[rate]] of something, regarded as part of a total of 100; a part of a whole.
#: {{syn|en|centage}}
#: {{ux|en|A high '''percentage''' of secondary school leavers take a gap year.}}
#* {{quote-journal|1=en|author=Walter Updegrave|title=How much income will you actually need in retirement?|magazine=w:CNN Money|url=https://money.cnn.com/2017/11/29/retirement/retirement-income/index.html|date=2017-11-29|passage=The replacement '''percentages''' are based largely on household spending data from the Department of Labor's Consumer Expenditure Survey.|accessdate=2 June 2025|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20250602174751/https://money.cnn.com/2017/11/29/retirement/retirement-income/index.html|archivedate=2 June 2025}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Julia Carpenter and Jackie Wattles|title=California has a new law: No more all-male boards|magazine=w:CNN Business|url=http://cnn.com/2018/09/30/business/california-requires-women-board-of-directors/index.html|date=2018-09-30|passage=Kramer said California’s legislation is weak compared to the laws in Norway and other European countries, which require a certain '''percentage''' of women on boards.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20250602225603/https://www.cnn.com/2018/09/30/business/california-requires-women-board-of-directors/index.html|archivedate=2 June 2025}}
# A share of the sales, profits, [[gross margin]] or similar.
#: {{ux|en|She gets a '''percentage''' for every vacuum cleaner sold.}}
# {{lb|en|informal}} [[benefit|Benefit]] or [[advantage]].
#: {{ux|en|There was no '''percentage''' in staying at home.}}

====Usage notes====
* A percentage is often denoted by the character {{angbr|[[%]]}}.
: e.g. ''50%'' denotes ''50 percent''.
* For sports statistics, percentages are mostly expressed in American and Canadian English as proportions of up to three decimal places and are read as if they are whole numbers.
: e.g. ''(0).500'' is pronounced ''five hundred'', not ''50%'' or ''50 percent''.

